On **Thursday 5 November 2026**, the **Bank of England** raised its main interest rate, called **Bank Rate**, from **3.75% to 4%**. It is the **first rise in over three years**.

The Bank said it raised rates because **prices are rising faster** than it wants. It warned that inflation could get close to **5%**, partly because of higher energy costs.

For many people, a quarter of a percent may not sound like much. But if money is already tight, any extra cost matters. This guide explains what could change, what will not, and where to get help.

{% callout type="info" title="The short version" %}
- Your **benefits are not cut** by the rate rise
- **Variable and tracker mortgages** are likely to cost more soon
- **Loans, credit cards and overdrafts** may get more expensive
- **Savings rates** may rise, but often slowly
- Free, confidential **debt advice** is available if you are struggling
{% /callout %}

## What is Bank Rate?

**Bank Rate** is the interest rate the Bank of England sets. It affects the rates banks and lenders charge when you **borrow**, and the rates they pay when you **save**.

When prices rise quickly, the Bank may raise Bank Rate to try to slow that down. The aim is to bring inflation back down over time. But in the short term, it can make borrowing more expensive.

## Will it affect my benefits?

**No.** Disability and health benefits, such as **PIP**, **DLA**, **Attendance Allowance**, **ESA**, **Carer's Allowance** and **Universal Credit**, are **not linked** to Bank Rate. The rise does not reduce them.

Most benefits usually go up each April in line with inflation. Higher inflation now could mean a bigger rise next April, but the April 2027 rates **have not been confirmed** yet. Read [the Autumn Budget 2026 and disabled people](/articles/budget-2026-disabled-people-and-benefits/) for the latest on benefit rates.

The bigger worry for many people is not their income, but **what they pay out**.

## Mortgages

- **Tracker and variable rate mortgages** usually go up quickly, often from your next monthly payment. Your lender should write to you.
- **Fixed rate mortgages** do not change until your fixed deal ends. If your deal ends soon, start looking at new deals early, as rates may be higher than before.

### If you get benefits and own your home

Universal Credit does **not** include help with mortgage payments in the same way it helps with rent. You may be able to get **Support for Mortgage Interest (SMI)**. It is a **loan**, not a grant, and it is paid back when you sell your home or move. Check GOV.UK, and get free advice before you agree to it.

{% callout type="warn" title="Struggling with your mortgage?" %}
Talk to your lender **as soon as possible**, before you miss a payment. Lenders must treat you fairly and look at options, such as a payment break or extending your mortgage term. Free debt advice can help you prepare.
{% /callout %}

## Rent

The rate rise does **not** directly change your rent. Your landlord cannot put the rent up just because interest rates have risen. Some landlords may try to pass on their own higher costs over time, but rent increases must follow your tenancy agreement and the law.

If your rent goes up and you get **Universal Credit** or **Housing Benefit**, tell the DWP or your council straight away, as your help with housing costs may change. If there is a gap between your rent and your housing support, you can ask your council for a **Discretionary Housing Payment**.

## Loans, credit cards and overdrafts

- **Credit cards, overdrafts and some loans** may become more expensive, especially those with variable rates.
- **Fixed rate loans** usually stay the same until they end.
- Be very careful with **buy now, pay later** and **high-cost credit**. They can quickly add up when money is tight.

If you are using credit to pay for essentials such as food, energy or rent, it is a sign to **get free debt advice now**.

## Savings

Banks may **slowly** raise the interest they pay on savings. If you have savings, it can be worth checking whether you could get a better rate.

### Savings and Universal Credit

If you get **Universal Credit**, your savings can affect your payment:

- **Under £6,000:** your savings do not affect Universal Credit
- **£6,000 to £16,000:** Universal Credit counts some assumed income from your savings, which reduces your payment
- **Over £16,000:** you usually cannot get Universal Credit

For couples, both partners' savings are added together. Other benefits, such as **PIP**, **DLA**, **Attendance Allowance** and **New Style ESA**, are **not** affected by your savings.

## Why this hits disabled people harder

Many disabled people and people with long-term conditions already face **higher everyday costs**, such as heating, equipment, transport and special diets. When prices and borrowing costs rise together, there is less room in the budget for anything extra.

That is why it matters to **claim everything you are entitled to**, and to get help early.

## What you can do now

1. **Check you are getting every benefit you can.** Many people miss out. Try our free [PIP self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/) and [NHS health costs check](/support/health-costs/checker/).
2. **Cut your bills where you can.** See [money off bills](/support/money-off-bills/), including [council tax discounts](/support/money-off-bills/council-tax/), [energy and water help](/support/money-off-bills/energy-and-water/) and [phone and broadband social tariffs](/support/money-off-bills/phone-broadband-and-tv/).
3. **Check your mortgage or loan.** Find out if your rate is fixed or variable, and when any fixed deal ends.
4. **Ask for emergency help if you need it.** In England, your council may help through the **Crisis and Resilience Fund**. In Wales, you can apply to the **Discretionary Assistance Fund**.
5. **Get free debt advice early.** Free, confidential help is available from **StepChange**, **Citizens Advice** and **National Debtline**. They will never judge you.

{% callout type="danger" title="If money worries are affecting your mental health" %}
Money worries can make anxiety and depression worse. If you are struggling to cope, call **Samaritans** free, any time, on **116 123**. If you are in danger, call **999**.
{% /callout %}
